From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cocoa: a display leaving the VM's Space stays left

What: an extra display's window that becomes key hands the keyboard back
to the main window only while the main window's Space shows.

Why: with two displays, the escape combo moved the pointer's display to
the macOS Space beside the VM's, then it flicked straight back (user
report, 3.0.0 candidate, 2026-10-06). When the main window's Space leaves
its display, macOS makes the app's other window (on the other display)
key; that window gave the key back to the main window, which is on a Space
not shown, and macOS switched the display back to it to show it.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's omacvm-cocoa-displays.patch).
Test: Tests/display/test-fullscreen-space.sh (build time) and the live
two-display check on the Mac mini (tracks: fullscreen-space). Applies
after omacvm-cocoa-fullscreen-own-space.patch.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -6557,12 +6557,16 @@ - (NSApplicationPresentationOptions)window:(NSWindow *)window
  * A click on another display's window makes it key. The keyboard and the
  * main window's pointer tracking belong to the main window: hand it back.
  * The click itself still reaches this window's view.
+ * Only while the main window's Space shows: when the escape combo (or a
+ * swipe) moves its display to another Space, macOS makes this window key,
+ * and making the main window key then would switch that display straight
+ * back to the VM's Space.
  */
 - (void)windowDidBecomeKey:(NSNotification *)note
 {
     dispatch_async(dispatch_get_main_queue(), ^{
         NSWindow *main = [cocoaView window];
-        if ([main isVisible] && [NSApp isActive] && ![main isKeyWindow]) {
+        if ([main isVisible] && [main isOnActiveSpace] && [NSApp isActive] && ![main isKeyWindow]) {
             [main makeKeyWindow];
         }
     });
